course: politics
lesson: 22
title: Guns, school safety and arguments about schools
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A lockdown drill is like a fire drill: everyone practises staying quiet behind a locked door so they would know what to do, even though real emergencies are very rare and most children never have one.
explain: Countries have very different rules about guns. Picture Hannah from Manchester seeing guns for sale next to the fishing rods in a Texas shop: what feels normal at home can look strange abroad, and the other way round.
explain: Many school arguments are about the same few things: safety, freedom, fairness, and who decides — parents, teachers, school boards, governments or children themselves.
explain: Both sides usually say they want children safe and treated fairly; they disagree about how. Hearing each side's best reasons is how you understand the argument, whichever side you end up on.
explain: Words from the scenes: «y'all» (US South) = you all, «fixin' to» = about to; «lowkey» = a bit, quietly; «sus» = suspicious; «muck» (Ireland) = rubbish; «bruv» (London) = mate; «our kid» (Manchester) = my brother or sister, «mardy» = sulky, «coppers» = police.
fact: Schools practise lockdown drills so everyone knows what to do in an emergency; real emergencies are very rare.
fact: Guns are much more common in the United States than in most countries, and many Americans own them for hunting, sport or protection.
fact: The Second Amendment of the US Constitution protects a right to keep and bear arms, and Americans argue about what that allows.
fact: After a mass shooting at Port Arthur in Tasmania in 1996, Australia agreed strict gun laws, banned many kinds of guns and paid people to hand in hundreds of thousands of them.
fact: Many studies report that Australia has had far fewer mass shootings since 1996, though people argue about whether other countries could copy it.
fact: In the UK most guns are tightly controlled and most police officers do not carry guns.
fact: Ideas for school safety include locked doors, guards, metal detectors, more counsellors and, in some US states, trained staff allowed to be armed; each idea has supporters and critics.
fact: Many schools in Ireland, the UK, Australia, New Zealand and parts of the US and Canada have banned or limited phones during the school day, news reports said.
fact: In many US school districts parents and school boards have argued about removing some library books; some call it checking books for age, others call it banning.
fact: In 2020 footballer Marcus Rashford, who had free school meals as a boy, campaigned for food help for poorer children in the holidays, and the UK government changed its plans, news reports said.
fact: In 2019 Quebec passed a law stopping some public workers, including public school teachers, from wearing religious symbols at work; it has been challenged in court.
fact: Australia passed a law, starting in December 2025, saying big social media apps must stop under-16s from having accounts, news reports said.
exercise: Why does Grace's school in Ohio have lockdown drills? | So everyone knows what to do if there is ever danger | Because the police have warned that a dangerous person is coming tomorrow | To punish children who talked in class | To test whether the classroom lights work
exercise: Are real school emergencies common? | No, they are very rare | Yes, most children have one every year | Yes, every school has one each week | No, because they have never happened anywhere
exercise: What does the Second Amendment of the US Constitution protect? | A right to keep and bear arms | A right to free school meals | A right to skip school on Fridays | A right to a social media account
exercise: Dylan's aunt wants stricter gun laws and his uncle says guns aren't the problem. What do both say they want? | Children to be safe | More guns in every classroom | No laws about anything at all | An end to all hunting forever
exercise: What did Australia do after the shooting at Port Arthur in 1996? | Made strict gun laws and paid people to hand in guns | Gave every adult in Australia a free gun to keep at home for safety | Banned fishing rods in all shops | Closed every school in Tasmania
exercise: Why was Hannah from Manchester surprised in a Texas shop? | In the UK guns are tightly controlled, so seeing them on sale was new | Guns are sold in every UK supermarket, next to the bread and the milk | Texas shops sell only guns and nothing else | It is illegal to sell fishing rods in Texas
exercise: Which idea have some people suggested for school safety in the US? | Metal detectors at the doors | Taking all the doors off schools | Closing every school for good | Banning teachers from locking doors
exercise: Why do some people oppose arming teachers? | They say more guns in school means more risk of accidents and a prison feel | They say teachers are not allowed to hold anything heavier than a pen or a book | They say guns make children cleverer | They say counsellors are more dangerous than guns
exercise: Why do some schools ban phones during the day? | They hope pupils will concentrate more and be kinder online | Phones make the school building colder | Phones are illegal for everyone everywhere in Ireland, so schools have to ban them | Teachers want to keep the phones themselves
exercise: Why do some parents dislike phone bans? | They want to reach their children in an emergency | They want children to text their friends during maths lessons | They think phones help children grow taller | They think phones are made at school
exercise: Amira's library took some books off the shelf to be looked at. Why do people argue about it? | Some say parents should check books for age; others say removing them is unfair to readers | Everyone agrees all such books should be burned at once, without anyone reading them first | Everyone agrees no book should ever be checked | Libraries are not allowed to keep any books
exercise: Why did footballer Marcus Rashford campaign about school meals? | He had needed free school meals as a boy | He owned a chain of school canteens | He wanted footballers to eat lunch at school | The government asked him to sell sandwiches
exercise: What does the 2019 Quebec law stop some public workers, including teachers, from doing at work? | Wearing religious symbols | Speaking French in class or in the staff room | Wearing shoes inside the school building | Eating lunch with the pupils at school
exercise: What does Australia's law starting in December 2025 say about social media? | Big apps must stop under-16s having accounts | Children must post something every day | Only adults over 30 may use the apps, and teenagers must use the phone instead | Phones are banned for everyone in Australia
exercise: Why do some people dislike the Australian social media law? | Teens may get round it, and age checks may mean sharing private information | It forces children to use social media more, for at least an hour every single day | It bans all phones in Australia | It makes the internet free for adults only
test: Hannah's Texan cousin shrugs: «y'all have no guns in shops? we're fixin' to buy bait right next to 'em, that's just normal round here.» What is true? | Gun rules differ a lot between countries, and each grew from its own history | Guns are sold the same way in every country | The UK sells guns beside fishing rods too | Texas has no rules at all about guns, so anyone of any age can buy one anywhere
test: Grace whispers: «lockdown drill again, lowkey scary, does that mean something bad's gonna happen?» What is the best answer? | No, it's practice, like a fire drill, so you'd stay calm if it ever came | Yes, a drill means danger is coming that day | Yes, drills only happen after a warning from the police that danger is near | No, drills are just a game to waste time
test: A friend says: «Australia banned every single gun in 1996.» How do you correct it? | Many kinds were banned and bought back, but some guns are still allowed with a licence | Right, nobody in Australia may own any gun at all, not even farmers with a licence | Australia handed out more guns in 1996 | Australia has never had any gun laws
test: An older boy in Cork fumes: «phone ban? that's pure muck, the teachers are a bunch of eejits.» What is the fairest point? | People disagree: some say bans help focus, others say phones help in emergencies | Phone bans are against the law in Ireland | Everyone agrees phones should be allowed all day | Teachers banned phones so they could sell them and keep all the money for the staff room
test: A girl says «they're banning books!» and a boy says «they're just checking them!» Which statement is fair? | Some books were taken off to be reviewed, and people disagree about whether that is fair | Every book in Florida was banned forever | Nobody disagrees about library books | Libraries never remove any book for any reason, so the girl and the boy are both making it up
test: Sam's mate in London asks: «bruv, why's a footballer going on about school dinners?» What is the best answer? | He'd needed free meals as a kid and campaigned for hungry children | Footballers must cook school dinners by law | He wanted to sell his own brand of lunches | It was just an advert for his football club's new kit and nothing to do with food
test: A teenager says: «that Quebec law is so sus, why can't a teacher wear a headscarf?» Which is the fair summary? | Supporters say teachers should look neutral; opponents say it unfairly stops people of some faiths | Everyone agrees the law is perfect | The law bans headscarves but allows crosses | The law bans every worker in all of Canada from wearing religious symbols, at work and at home
test: Mia's brother in Perth moans: «Mum reckons the under-16 ban's about time. I reckon it's bloody unfair.» Which is fair? | Some say it protects kids from harm; others say teens will get round it and lose contact | It is a ban on phones for everyone | It means adults can't use social media | Nobody in Australia has said anything good about it, not even a single parent
test: In an action film a teacher fights off a whole gang single-handed. What is the real-world argument? | Some say trained armed staff could help; others say more guns in school means more risk | Every teacher in the US already carries a gun, so the film shows a normal school day | Films show exactly how school safety works | Armed teachers are required in the UK
test: Dinner goes silent after Dylan's aunt and uncle argue about guns. What is the best thing to remember? | When both want children safe, the argument is about how to get there | One of them must want children harmed | Gun laws are not something people disagree about | Only politicians are allowed to talk about guns
test: Hannah messages from Manchester: «our kid's well mardy, says UK coppers carry guns everywhere.» What is true? | Most UK police officers do not carry guns | Every UK police officer carries a gun | UK police officers carry swords instead of guns | UK police are not allowed handcuffs
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a lockdown drill in a school. | To ensure that all persons know how to act should a danger ever arise | To signify that a danger is anticipated upon that particular day | To discipline pupils for misconduct in class | To ascertain the proper functioning of the electric lighting and the fire alarms
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Australia's response to the shooting at Port Arthur in 1996. | Strict national firearms laws were adopted and a substantial quantity of guns was purchased back by government | All restrictions upon firearms were subsequently abolished | Every school in Tasmania was permanently closed | Each adult citizen was issued a firearm by the state
exam: State the matter protected by the Second Amendment to the Constitution of the United States. | A right to keep and bear arms | A right to free school meals for all pupils | A prohibition upon religious symbols worn by teachers | A prohibition upon social media accounts for persons under sixteen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the legislation enacted in Australia commencing in December 2025? | Large social media platforms are required to prevent persons under sixteen from holding accounts | All mobile telephones are prohibited throughout the Commonwealth | Persons under sixteen are required to hold social media accounts | Social media platforms are prohibited for persons over sixteen
